import pandas as pd

def group_rates(df, threshold):
    rows = {}
    for g, part in df.groupby("group"):
        y = part["repaid"].astype(bool).to_numpy()
        ok = (part["score"] >= threshold).to_numpy()
        rows[g] = {"approved": ok.mean(), "TPR": ok[y].mean(), "FPR": ok[~y].mean(), "PPV": y[ok].mean()}
    return pd.DataFrame(rows).T[["approved", "TPR", "FPR", "PPV"]]

exercises.solution:
Each rate is a mean of a Boolean array restricted to the right people:

```python
import pandas as pd

def group_rates(df, threshold):
    rows = {}
    for g, part in df.groupby("group"):
        y = part["repaid"].astype(bool).to_numpy()
        ok = (part["score"] >= threshold).to_numpy()
        rows[g] = {"approved": ok.mean(), "TPR": ok[y].mean(),
                   "FPR": ok[~y].mean(), "PPV": y[ok].mean()}
    return pd.DataFrame(rows).T[["approved", "TPR", "FPR", "PPV"]]
```

For group A in the tests, scores 0.9, 0.8, 0.7 are approved at threshold 0.65: approval 3/5; of the three repayers (0.9, 0.8, 0.6) two are approved, TPR 2/3; of the two defaulters (0.7, 0.4) one is approved, FPR 1/2; of the three approved, two repaid, PPV 2/3. In real audits, also report the number of people behind each rate: a TPR computed from 30 people has a wide confidence interval, and gaps between small groups may be noise.
